  "final_text": "Short answer\n- Pooled (meta-analytic across NHANES cycles, model 6 = age + sex + race/ethnicity): small positive association between serum PFOA and total cholesterol, borderline significant.\n  • Beta ≈ +0.060 SD of cholesterol per 1 SD PFOA; p ≈ 0.050\n  • Between-cycle heterogeneity is substantial: I² ≈ 56%\n- Within-cycle estimates: mixed and heterogeneous.\n  • Many cycle-specific estimates are positive (some statistically significant), but a sizable fraction are null or negative.\n  • Roughly 55% of cycle estimates align with the pooled positive direction; ≈45% do not.\n  • No Simpson’s paradox detected: the pooled direction does not reverse relative to the majority of cycles, but the effect is not consistent across time.\n\nInterpretation and caveats\n- Evidence suggests, at most, a weak positive PFOA→cholesterol association in NHANES, but it varies by cycle and is not robust.\n- Confounding is a concern:\n  • Serum albumin is strongly positively associated with PFOA (β ≈ +0.18, p < 0.001), consistent with protein-binding confounding; failing to adjust can inflate PFOA–lipid associations.\n  • There is also a positive correlation of PFOA with cholesterol itself (lipid partitioning).\n- Temporal trends can bias pooled results because PFOA declined markedly over time while cholesterol and treatment patterns changed; analyzing within cycles (as above) helps, but heterogeneity remains.\n\nWhat to do if you need a definitive estimate\n- Refit within each cycle (or small cycle blocks) and meta-analyze, adjusting for:\n  • Albumin, BMI, smoking/cotinine, kidney function (creatinine), socioeconomic proxies\n  • Lipid-lowering medication use and diagnosed dyslipidemia (exclude or adjust)\n- Check non-linearity and compare across PFAS (PFOS, PFHxS).\n- Run disease/medication sensitivity analyses; our automated sensitivity run failed on this server, but it’s important for lipid outcomes.\n\nBottom line\nIn NHANES, serum PFOA shows a small, borderline positive pooled association with total cholesterol, but cycle-specific estimates are heterogeneous with many null/negative cycles. No Simpson’s paradox is evident, yet the instability across time and strong albumin/lipid confounding mean the population evidence for a consistent positive effect is weak and should be interpreted cautiously.",
